### What is the Single-Row Subset Sum Optimization?

This problem is the same as the classic Subset Sum but focuses on the optimized single-row approach. Instead of a 2D table, we use a single boolean array and iterate in reverse. This reduces space from O(N * S) to O(S) while preserving the O(N * S) time complexity.

At National Bank of India, the settlement team needs to check if any combination of invoice amounts can exactly match an outstanding vendor payment.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SubsetSumOptimized {
    public static boolean canSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[target + 1];
        dp[0] = true;

        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int s = target; s >= num; s--) {
                if (dp[s - num]) dp[s] = true;
            }
            if (dp[target]) return true;  // early exit
        }
        return dp[target];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] invoices = {3000, 34000, 4000, 12000, 5000, 2000};
        int outstanding = 9000;
        System.out.println("Can settle " + outstanding + ": " + canSum(invoices, outstanding));
        // 4000 + 5000 = 9000 -> true
    }
}
```

```text
Can settle 9000: true
```

### Why Does Reverse Iteration Ensure Each Number Is Used Once?

When we iterate from target down to the number, we read `dp[s - num]` which still holds the value from before the current number was processed. If we iterated from low to high, `dp[s - num]` might already reflect the current number being added, effectively allowing the same number to be used multiple times.

This is the fundamental distinction between 0/1 Knapsack (high-to-low) and Unbounded Knapsack (low-to-high).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ReverseIterationProof {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int num = 5000;
        int target = 15000;

        // HIGH-TO-LOW (correct for 0/1): each number used once
        boolean[] dp1 = new boolean[target + 1];
        dp1[0] = true;
        for (int s = target; s >= num; s--) {
            if (dp1[s - num]) dp1[s] = true;
        }
        System.out.println("High-to-low dp[15000] = " + dp1[15000]);  // false (only one 5000)
        System.out.println("High-to-low dp[5000]  = " + dp1[5000]);   // true

        // LOW-TO-HIGH (wrong for 0/1): allows reuse
        boolean[] dp2 = new boolean[target + 1];
        dp2[0] = true;
        for (int s = num; s <= target; s++) {
            if (dp2[s - num]) dp2[s] = true;
        }
        System.out.println("Low-to-high dp[15000] = " + dp2[15000]);  // true (5000 used 3 times)
    }
}
```

```text
High-to-low dp[15000] = false
High-to-low dp[5000]  = true
Low-to-high dp[15000] = true
```

### How Does This Compare to the 2D Table Approach?

The 2D approach uses `dp[i][s]` where i is the number of items considered and s is the sum. The 1D approach compresses this by observing that row i depends only on row i-1, so a single row suffices when swept in the right direction.

| Aspect | 2D Table | 1D Optimized |
|---|---|---|
| Space | O(N * S) | O(S) |
| Time | O(N * S) | O(N * S) |
| Sweep direction | Left-to-right in each row | Right-to-left (high-to-low) |
| Reconstruction | Trace back through table | Not possible without extra storage |
| Early exit | Check dp[N][S] at end | Check dp[S] after each number |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSum2DFull {
    public static boolean canSum2D(int[] nums, int target) {
        int n = nums.length;
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[n + 1][target + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = true;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int s = 1; s <= target; s++) {
                dp[i][s] = dp[i - 1][s];  // skip this number
                if (s >= nums[i - 1]) {
                    dp[i][s] = dp[i][s] || dp[i - 1][s - nums[i - 1]];  // use this number
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[n][target];
    }
}
```

### What Is the Recursive Brute-Force Approach?

Before DP, the recursive approach tries including or excluding each number. This has exponential time O(2^N) but is useful for understanding the subproblem structure.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumRecursive {
    public static boolean canSum(int[] nums, int index, int remaining) {
        if (remaining == 0) return true;   // found a valid subset
        if (index >= nums.length || remaining < 0) return false;

        // Include nums[index] OR exclude it
        return canSum(nums, index + 1, remaining - nums[index])
            || canSum(nums, index + 1, remaining);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] invoices = {3000, 4000, 5000};
        System.out.println(canSum(invoices, 0, 9000));  // true (4000 + 5000)
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### What Are the Edge Cases?

Edge cases include: target is zero (always true), single number equal to target (true), all numbers larger than target (false unless target is zero), and target exceeds total sum (immediately false).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumOptEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(SubsetSumOptimized.canSum(new int[]{1, 2}, 0));    // true
        System.out.println(SubsetSumOptimized.canSum(new int[]{7}, 7));       // true
        System.out.println(SubsetSumOptimized.canSum(new int[]{10, 20}, 5));  // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
```

### What About Non-Negative Integers Including Zero?

The title says non-negative, meaning zero can appear. A zero in the input does not change reachability: adding zero to any sum gives the same sum. However, it does affect the count of subsets (if counting), since including or excluding zero gives different subsets with the same sum.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumWithZero {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Zero does not change reachability
        int[] nums = {0, 3, 5};
        System.out.println(SubsetSumOptimized.canSum(nums, 8));  // true (3 + 5)
        System.out.println(SubsetSumOptimized.canSum(nums, 0));  // true (empty or {0})
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
```

### What About Counting Subsets Instead of Boolean?

Changing the boolean array to an integer count array turns the decision problem into a counting problem. The transition becomes `dp[s] += dp[s - num]` instead of `dp[s] = dp[s] || dp[s - num]`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumCount {
    public static int countSubsets(int[] nums, int target) {
        int[] dp = new int[target + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;
        for (int num : nums) {
            for (int s = target; s >= num; s--) {
                dp[s] += dp[s - num];
            }
        }
        return dp[target];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        System.out.println("Subsets summing to 5: " + countSubsets(amounts, 5));
    }
}
```

```text
Subsets summing to 5: 3
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Equal Partition: can the array be split into two equal halves? Reduce to Subset Sum with target = total/2.
- Minimum Subset Sum Difference: find the partition with smallest difference.
- Target Sum with +/-: assign + or - to each number to reach a target.
- Count of subsets: change boolean to count.
- Reconstruct the subset: use the 2D table.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetSumFollowUps {
    // Boolean -> Count: change || to +=
    // Reachable -> Optimal: combine with value tracking
    // 0/1 -> Unbounded: change sweep direction to low-to-high
}
```
